```
hint: Inside quotes, `name` is only a word. Without quotes, it's the variable.
hint: `age + 1` is worked out before print shows it.
hint: `+` joins `"Mia"` and `"!"` with no space.
right: Line 3 prints the word `name` and then the value `Mia`. Line 4 works out `9 + 1` and prints `age: 10`. Line 5 joins two strings: `Mia!`.
wrong: Quotes make text, so `"name"` prints the word name, while `name` without quotes gives `Mia`. `age + 1` is calculated first, giving 10. And `+` joins the strings `Mia` and `!` with no space.
wrong[Mia Mia\nage: 10\nMia!]: The first value on line 3 is `"name"` in quotes, so it's the word name, not the variable.
wrong[name Mia\nage: 9 1\nMia!]: `age + 1` has no comma: it's one calculation, 9 + 1, which gives 10.
wrong[name Mia\nage: 10\nMia !]: `+` joins strings exactly as they are, without a space: `Mia!`.

=== task: parsons hard
title: Swap two colors
prompt: Arrange lines so that the program prints:
```output
blue red
```
The program must first store `"red"` in `a` and `"blue"` in `b`, then swap them using a temporary variable. Not every line is needed.
lines:
```python
a = "red"
b = "blue"
temp = a
a = b
b = temp
print(a, b)
```
distractors:
```python
b = a
a = temp
```
hint: Before `a` gets a new value, save its old value somewhere.
hint: The swap is three moves: save `a` in `temp`, copy `b` into `a`, then copy `temp` into `b`.
right: `temp = a` saves `"red"`, `a = b` copies `"blue"` into `a`, and `b = temp` puts the saved `"red"` into `b`. No value is lost, so the print shows `blue red`.
wrong: Create both variables first, then do the three moves in order: `temp = a` (save red), `a = b` (a becomes blue), `b = temp` (b becomes red). `b = a` would copy blue into b after a has already changed, so red would be lost.

=== task: code hard
title: Rotate three values
prompt: The starter code creates three variables. Rotate their values to the left, so that:
- `a` ends up with the value `b` had (`"two"`)
- `b` ends up with the value `c` had (`"three"`)
- `c` ends up with the value `a` had (`"one"`)

Move the values with variables (one temporary variable is enough). Don't type the strings again.
starter:
```python
a = "one"
b = "two"
c = "three"
# rotate the values below
```
solution:
```python
a = "one"
b = "two"
c = "three"
temp = a
a = b
b = c
c = temp
```
tests:
- a
- b
- c
fail[1]: `a` should end up with `b`'s old value, `"two"`.
fail[2]: `b` should end up with `c`'s old value, `"three"`. Check the order of your lines: if `c` changes before `b` copies it, the value is gone.
fail[3]: `c` should end up with `a`'s old value, `"one"`. Save `a` in a temporary variable before `a` changes, then copy it into `c` at the end.
forbid[a="two"]: Move the values with variables instead of typing the strings again.
forbid[b="three"]: Move the values with variables instead of typing the strings again.
forbid[c="one"]: Move the values with variables instead of typing the strings again.
forbid[a='two']: Move the values with variables instead of typing the strings again.
forbid[b='three']: Move the values with variables instead of typing the strings again.
forbid[c='one']: Move the values with variables instead of typing the strings again.
hint: Like a swap, the first value that gets overwritten must be saved first.
hint: Save `a` in `temp`. Then `a` can take `b`'s value, and `b` can take `c`'s value.
hint: Finish with `c = temp`.
right: Saving `a` first means nothing is lost: `a = b` and `b = c` each copy a value that's still there, and `c = temp` brings back the saved `"one"`. One temporary variable is all a rotation needs.
wrong: The value of `a` is overwritten first, so save it: `temp = a`. Then shift the others along: `a = b`, then `b = c`. Finally `c = temp` puts the saved value at the end. Doing `b = c` before `a = b` would lose `b`'s value.

